<TopicPage slug="subjects/neuroscience/intermediate/the-action-potential">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>action potential</strong> is the brain's universal bit — a millisecond electrical
    spike, identical in every neuron, carrying information by frequency and timing. Hodgkin and
    Huxley decoded its mechanism in 1952 (squid axons, voltage clamp, and one of biology's
    greatest papers) — a story of ion channels, batteries and feedback that the entire field
    still builds on.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the spike, phase by phase — and the pump that resets it" widget="equationStep" config={ap2Cfg} />

  <h2>Consequences worth carrying</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Frequency coding:</strong> since spikes are uniform, intensity is encoded in rate — a bright light makes optic neurons fire faster, not "harder".</li>
    <li><strong>Refractory periods set the speed limit</strong> and force one-way travel — the spike can't re-excite the segment behind it.</li>
    <li><strong>Local anaesthetics block sodium channels:</strong> lidocaine numbs by silencing spikes in the region — pharmacology meeting channel physics.</li>
    <li><strong>Scorpions and pufferfish knew the channels first:</strong> their toxins target specific sodium channel gates — toxins as research tools, a whole subfield's method.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Hodgkin & Huxley, J. Physiol. (1952); Nobel 1963; Kandel et al., Principles of Neural Science.</p>
</TopicPage>
